By campfire on the trail when day is done,

157
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:22,000
By campfire on the trail when day is done,

158
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:28,000
Write me a letter, send it by name,

159
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:34,000
Email, that is, folks. And here's an answer to one of your emails:

160
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:42,000
Music,

161
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:48,000
I heard from Rick Ferry, who's an assistant scoutmaster with Troop 670 in Manassas, Virginia,

162
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:53,000
And he wrote in to say: do you have a thought or philosophy on first year patrols?

163
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:58,000
In the past, our unit has simply integrated new scouts into existing patrols.

164
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:05,000
In doing so, we haven't adequately met the fundamental development of skills needed by these scouts to thrive.

165
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:12,000
We are now installing a dedicated first year program where scouts coming from various levels of developed skill sets,

166
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:17,000
as weblos, engage in the first year program as a cohort based on the patrol method.

167
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:19,000
What guidance can you share?

168
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:26,000
Well, Rick, I'm a pretty hard sell on things like first year programs and things like that.

169
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:32,000
I think the only relevant issue is what these new scouts want,

170
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:38,000
And you'll find out what they want and what you want are going to be pretty much the same things.

171
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:40,000
I wouldn't tell them what you want.

172
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:43,000
I would begin by asking them some questions.

173
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:49,000
If you have a group of weblos transitioning into your troop or you have several new recruits there,

174
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:51,000
I would sit down with them and I'd say so.

175
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:57,000
Do you, fellas, want to stay together as a patrol or would you rather be split up amongst the other patrols?

176
00:14:57,000 --> 00:15:01,000
Do you guys know what a patrol is? Do you know how it functions?

177
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:06,000
Do you want to elect a patrol leader amongst yourselves or do you want to choose an older scout to help you out?

178
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:09,000
What do you think your patrol ought to work on first?

179
00:15:09,000 --> 00:15:12,000
Have you looked at our schedule of camping trips?

180
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:15,000
Do you know what you're going to need to do to get prepared for those?

181
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:19,000
What kind of help would you need from the adults who are involved or the older scouts?

182
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:23,000
There's a pretty good chance when you ask these questions you're going to be met with a blank stare

183
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:27,000
because they have little idea about what you're talking about.

184
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:33,000
So ask them where they could learn the answers to those questions and have a scout handbook nearby,

185
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:38,000
And they won't have great answers at first, but with a little guidance they'll find some great answers

186
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:44,000
and they will be pretty sure that everything that happens from there on out is their idea.

187
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:51,000
They'll begin to ask those questions of themselves before too long and they'll find their own answers too.

188
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:57,000
So it begins by talking with the new scouts, finding out what they're really interested in,

189
00:15:57,000 --> 00:16:00,000
how they would like to arrange their patrol, what they think,

190
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:04,000
and getting them invested in the idea that they're leading themselves.

191
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:09,000
Like I said, I'm a pretty hard sell on something like a dedicated first year program

192
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:13,000
that's focused on basic skills and stuff like that.

193
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:19,000
I understand that that is a very common thing and I don't think that it's bad.

194
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:23,000
What I think is that our approach to it is a little messed up sometimes.

195
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:28,000
I think we can work with new scouts to help them create a plan of their own,

196
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:30,000
rather than imposing one on them.

197
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:32,000
I would start very small.

198
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:34,000
I'd stay one or two weeks ahead of them,

199
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:40,000
I'd check in often and I would make a big deal over the smallest victories their new patrol has.

200
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:43,000
When the going is rough, we're going to want to help them out.

201
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:46,000
Otherwise, we want to watch what happens.

202
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:47,000
We want to encourage them.

203
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:49,000
We want to help them discover answers.

204
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:53,000
We want to help them understand that they're leading themselves

205
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:56,000
and building their own experience and building their patrol.

206
00:16:56,000 --> 00:16:58,000
And it's amazing what happens

207
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:02,000
When you give them that latitude, you ask questions and you follow their lead

208
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:05,000
rather than going out front and leading them.

209
00:17:05,000 --> 00:17:10,000
I hope that makes sense but, like I said, a dedicated first year program-

210
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:13,000
meh, I'm a hard sell on that

211
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:16,000
If it's something that we come up with and dictate to them

212
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:21,000
more than something that they begin working on and discovering

213
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:23,000
and building for themselves.

214
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:25,000
This next email we're going to keep anonymous

215
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:28,000
and the reason for that will become clear as we read it.

216
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:34,000
Our scouts wouldn't even volunteer to be a candidate during our last leadership elections.

217
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:37,000
We are so fed up with this lack of interest

218
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:39,000
We were forced to appoint all the youth leaders.

219
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:42,000
What can we do to get the boys to step up

220
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:45,000
and take a more active role in leading the troop?

221
00:17:45,000 --> 00:17:50,000
Reading between the lines on that email, I don't see a scout problem here.

222
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:54,000
It's probably a problem with the attitude and expectations of the adults.

223
00:17:54,000 --> 00:17:58,000
I say this because you're telling me none, zero

224
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:02,000
scouts were interested in a leadership election,

225
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:06,000
And then you talk about being so fed up with these guys

226
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:11,000
and that you are forced to appoint them to leadership positions.

227
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:13,000
and good luck with that, by the way.

228
00:18:13,000 --> 00:18:16,000
I don't know that that's going to be a very good solution.

229
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:20,000
If nobody raised their hand, it sounds like being a youth leader

230
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:23,000
in your troop isn't a very good experience,

231
00:18:23,000 --> 00:18:26,000
And I think that has something to do with the attitude

232
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:28,000
and the expectations of the adults involved.

233
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:32,000
I'd guess there's no real leadership role to fulfill

234
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:35,000
because the adults are doing all the important things

235
00:18:35,000 --> 00:18:37,000
or they have some kind of a chip on their shoulder

236
00:18:37,000 --> 00:18:41,000
about exactly what leadership quote unquote- means,

237
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:43,000
And I understand how frustrating this can be,

238
00:18:43,000 --> 00:18:47,000
but I've never known scouts to refuse all the leadership positions

239
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:49,000
when there's the right spirit in the troop,

240
00:18:49,000 --> 00:18:53,000
So I have to assume, unfortunately, it's probably not them.

241
00:18:53,000 --> 00:18:56,000
Now, this is pretty plain talk. I understand,

242
00:18:56,000 --> 00:18:58,000
and I don't want to come off as Mr Know-it-all,

243
00:18:58,000 --> 00:19:02,000
but am I close to understanding the situation?

244
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:06,000
That was the first email reply, and the person responded.

245
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:08,000
maybe our expectations are a little too high.

246
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:12,000
It seems, though, that the scouts would rather just let the adults

247
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:14,000
do all the heavy lifting.

248
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:17,000
If the scouts do anything at all, their efforts are unorganized

249
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:20,000
and they don't take their responsibility seriously.

250
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:23,000
So my reply was this: scouts are unorganized

251
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:26,000
and they aren't very fond of planning in the way

252
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:28,000
that adults will find acceptable,

253
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:32,000
and they certainly will let the adults do all the heavy lifting

254
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:34,000
if the adults do it.

255
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:36,000
I mean, frankly, who wouldn't?

256
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:38,000
It's just good, plain common sense.

257
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:40,000
These are smart scouts.

258
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:42,000
If you want to do all the work, that's fine with me,

259
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:44,000
I'll just go along for the ride.

260
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:46,000
And, of course, they don't take their responsibility seriously.

261
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:48,000
They aren't 45 year old men,

262
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:50,000
They're a bunch of teenage boys

263
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:52,000
who are working at becoming scouts,

264
00:19:52,000 --> 00:19:55,000
So these sound like perfectly normal scouts

265
00:19:55,000 --> 00:19:57,000
and there's absolutely nothing wrong with them.

266
00:19:57,000 --> 00:20:00,000
It's apparently the way the adults are reacting,

267
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:03,000
their expectations and their attitude,

268
00:20:03,000 --> 00:20:06,000
and it's kind of actively keeping the scouts from leading.

269
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:09,000
Step one, I think, is getting the adults involved

270
00:20:09,000 --> 00:20:11,000
with the troop together, sitting down

271
00:20:11,000 --> 00:20:13,000
and getting real with each other

272
00:20:13,000 --> 00:20:15,000
over the current state of affairs

273
00:20:15,000 --> 00:20:17,000
And think about what you'll need to do

274
00:20:17,000 --> 00:20:20,000
to reset your attitude and your expectations.

275
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:22,000
Let me offer this to you as a way

276
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:24,000
of helping you shape your attitude. okay,

277
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:27,000
I have a splendid work of art

278
00:20:27,000 --> 00:20:30,000
on my refrigerator door from one of my favorite neighbors.

279
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:32,000
Now. she showed up at our door

280
00:20:32,000 --> 00:20:34,000
and proudly presented this work of art

281
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:36,000
to my wife the other day,

282
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:39,000
with the pride and pleasure only an eight year old can muster,

283
00:20:39,000 --> 00:20:42,000
And I should say this is only the latest

284
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:44,000
of many such gifts she's given us.

285
00:20:44,000 --> 00:20:46,000
Now nobody's ever going to mistake

286
00:20:46,000 --> 00:20:49,000
what's on our refrigerator door for the Mona Lisa.

287
00:20:49,000 --> 00:20:52,000
It's a crayon drawing from a little girl

288
00:20:52,000 --> 00:20:56,000
and it has little objective value as a work of art,

289
00:20:56,000 --> 00:20:59,000
But to my wife and I it's absolutely priceless

290
00:20:59,000 --> 00:21:02,000
and we wouldn't sell it to you for a million bucks.

291
00:21:02,000 --> 00:21:04,000
So now imagine what would happen

292
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:08,000
if, when our little neighbor girl knocked on the door

293
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:10,000
and handed the crayon drawing to my wife,

294
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:13,000
she had just balled it up and thrown it into the trash,

295
00:21:13,000 --> 00:21:15,000
We'd probably never see that little girl again,

296
00:21:15,000 --> 00:21:17,000
and rightfully so.

297
00:21:17,000 --> 00:21:19,000
She may go home and throw away all our crayons

298
00:21:19,000 --> 00:21:20,000
and never draw again.

299
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:22,000
That'd be pretty traumatic, wouldn't it?

300
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:24,000
How about if we had accepted

301
00:21:24,000 --> 00:21:25,000
the crayon drawing from her

302
00:21:25,000 --> 00:21:27,000
and then started reworking it

303
00:21:27,000 --> 00:21:29,000
as she watched us- okay,

304
00:21:29,000 --> 00:21:31,000
I mean, I'm a reasonably good artist

305
00:21:31,000 --> 00:21:34,000
and I could have made a number of improvements to her work.

306
00:21:34,000 --> 00:21:36,000
I wonder how she would have felt about that,

307
00:21:36,000 --> 00:21:39,000
and how rude would that have been

308
00:21:39,000 --> 00:21:42,000
to take that drawing and start reworking it

309
00:21:42,000 --> 00:21:45,000
and say, oh honey, you could have made this a lot better

310
00:21:45,000 --> 00:21:46,000
by doing this and this.

311
00:21:46,000 --> 00:21:48,000
No, it was a nice gift,

312
00:21:48,000 --> 00:21:51,000
It was something that she created and she gave to us.

313
00:21:51,000 --> 00:21:54,000
So we need to look at our Scouts' efforts

314
00:21:54,000 --> 00:21:57,000
in leadership the same way.

315
00:21:57,000 --> 00:21:59,000
When a Scout takes on a leadership role,

316
00:21:59,000 --> 00:22:02,000
his first efforts are not going to be all that great

317
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:04,000
because he's a teenage boy.

318
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:06,000
He's going to be sloppy and chaotic

319
00:22:06,000 --> 00:22:08,000
and he may barely make a dent.

320
00:22:08,000 --> 00:22:10,000
His plans are going to be terrible

321
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:12,000
and incomplete and lackluster

322
00:22:12,000 --> 00:22:14,000
and their skills are going to be fan-wanting,

323
00:22:14,000 --> 00:22:16,000
But you know what.

324
00:22:16,000 --> 00:22:18,000
They have done something.

325
00:22:18,000 --> 00:22:22,000
So we congratulate them on the smallest triumph.

326
00:22:22,000 --> 00:22:25,000
We commiserate with them when things fall apart

327
00:22:25,000 --> 00:22:27,000
and we don't point out

328
00:22:27,000 --> 00:22:29,000
everything that they failed to do.

329
00:22:29,000 --> 00:22:32,000
No, we ask them questions

330
00:22:32,000 --> 00:22:35,000
that help them discover how to do better.

331
00:22:35,000 --> 00:22:37,000
If we build on this a little at a time,

332
00:22:37,000 --> 00:22:40,000
with a lot of patience, respectful of their efforts,

333
00:22:40,000 --> 00:22:43,000
not throwing them out, not reworking them-

334
00:22:43,000 --> 00:22:46,000
they will grow in confidence and skill.

335
00:22:46,000 --> 00:22:50,000
Now what's important is not how slick our program is

336
00:22:50,000 --> 00:22:52,000
or how orderly our meetings are.

337
00:22:52,000 --> 00:22:55,000
What's important is a Scout's effort

338
00:22:55,000 --> 00:22:58,000
at developing some leadership skill.

339
00:22:58,000 --> 00:23:01,000
The efforts are small and seemingly insignificant at first,

340
00:23:01,000 --> 00:23:03,000
but things will get better

341
00:23:03,000 --> 00:23:06,000
when they're met with an attitude of thanks

342
00:23:06,000 --> 00:23:09,000
and positive feedback and encouragement.

343
00:23:09,000 --> 00:23:11,000
Now you go through that process.

344
00:23:11,000 --> 00:23:13,000
Pretty soon you have a group of youth leaders

345
00:23:13,000 --> 00:23:15,000
who are firing on all eight cylinders

346
00:23:15,000 --> 00:23:19,000
and almost as soon as they get to that point,

347
00:23:19,000 --> 00:23:21,000
it's time for them to step aside

348
00:23:21,000 --> 00:23:23,000
and for the next bunch to step up

349
00:23:23,000 --> 00:23:25,000
And we'll start all over again.

350
00:23:25,000 --> 00:23:27,000
We never arrive right

351
00:23:27,000 --> 00:23:29,000
As Scouters. we never arrive.

352
00:23:29,000 --> 00:23:31,000
We just keep on traveling.

353
00:23:31,000 --> 00:23:33,000
And I want to repeat myself:

354
00:23:33,000 --> 00:23:35,000
What's important isn't how slick our program is

355
00:23:35,000 --> 00:23:37,000
or how orderly everything is

356
00:23:37,000 --> 00:23:39,000
or how well planned everything is.

357
00:23:39,000 --> 00:23:43,000
What's important is that Scouts are making some effort

358
00:23:43,000 --> 00:23:45,000
at developing leadership skills.

359
00:23:45,000 --> 00:23:49,000
That's the attitude that encourages them to raise their hand

360
00:23:49,000 --> 00:23:53,000
and that's what I want you to think about and work on

361
00:23:53,000 --> 00:23:55,000
and see what happens.

362
00:23:55,000 --> 00:23:57,000
See if that changes some things in your troop.

363
00:23:57,000 --> 00:23:59,000
Next up is an answer to an email I received

364
00:23:59,000 --> 00:24:01,000
from a new Scout Master.

365
00:24:01,000 --> 00:24:03,000
He's an Eagle Scout,

366
00:24:03,000 --> 00:24:06,000
He's been involved with Cubs for several years

367
00:24:06,000 --> 00:24:08,000
and he sent me a long email

368
00:24:08,000 --> 00:24:11,000
about the history of the troop that he's involved with

369
00:24:11,000 --> 00:24:14,000
and the adult leadership in the troop that he's involved with

370
00:24:14,000 --> 00:24:16,000
and his hopes of empowering the youth leaders

371
00:24:16,000 --> 00:24:18,000
to run their own troop.

372
00:24:18,000 --> 00:24:21,000
There's a few older Scouts and many younger Scouts in the troop

373
00:24:21,000 --> 00:24:24,000
and he's trying to wrap his head around what to do.

374
00:24:24,000 --> 00:24:26,000
This is the answer that I sent him.

375
00:24:26,000 --> 00:24:29,000
What he described in the history of the troop

376
00:24:29,000 --> 00:24:31,000
and the way the adults are

377
00:24:31,000 --> 00:24:33,000
is pretty ordinary.

378
00:24:33,000 --> 00:24:35,000
It's pretty familiar.

379
00:24:35,000 --> 00:24:38,000
Any troop has a history, good, bad or indifferent.

380
00:24:38,000 --> 00:24:41,000
Here's a couple of thoughts you may find useful

381
00:24:41,000 --> 00:24:44,000
As a new Scout Master. you can't try to fix everything at once.

382
00:24:44,000 --> 00:24:47,000
You have to rebuild the engine while the car is running.

383
00:24:47,000 --> 00:24:49,000
You can't take the engine out

384
00:24:49,000 --> 00:24:51,000
and put it up on blocks for a couple of months

385
00:24:51,000 --> 00:24:53,000
and meticulously rebuild it.

386
00:24:53,000 --> 00:24:55,000
You have to kind of pick one thing,

387
00:24:55,000 --> 00:24:57,000
focus on that for a while

388
00:24:57,000 --> 00:24:59,000
and then, once that's going well,

389
00:24:59,000 --> 00:25:01,000
move on to the next thing.

390
00:25:01,000 --> 00:25:03,000
I'm saying that this is an allegory

391
00:25:03,000 --> 00:25:05,000
and allegories fall apart after a while,

392
00:25:05,000 --> 00:25:07,000
but you understand what I mean.

393
00:25:07,000 --> 00:25:09,000
You can't put a stop to everything,

394
00:25:09,000 --> 00:25:11,000
draw a line and say, now it's a new day.

395
00:25:11,000 --> 00:25:13,000
You have to kind of work along

396
00:25:13,000 --> 00:25:15,000
with an operating troop

397
00:25:15,000 --> 00:25:18,000
and you'll fix things as they come up

398
00:25:18,000 --> 00:25:20,000
and you'll work on things as they come up,

399
00:25:20,000 --> 00:25:22,000
and the Scouts in leadership positions

400
00:25:22,000 --> 00:25:24,000
will be your great ally in this.

401
00:25:24,000 --> 00:25:26,000
Everybody will be overwhelmed

402
00:25:26,000 --> 00:25:28,000
if you try and do too much at once.

403
00:25:28,000 --> 00:25:31,000
The biggest change when a new Scoutmaster takes over

404
00:25:31,000 --> 00:25:33,000
is just kind of the general approach

405
00:25:33,000 --> 00:25:34,000
and the attitude,

406
00:25:34,000 --> 00:25:37,000
and that changed when you take over a Scoutmaster.

407
00:25:37,000 --> 00:25:39,000
It's already done

408
00:25:39,000 --> 00:25:41,000
So. now everything needs to catch up

409
00:25:41,000 --> 00:25:43,000
with that vision and that attitude

410
00:25:43,000 --> 00:25:45,000
and you'll build on things a little at a time.

411
00:25:45,000 --> 00:25:48,000
You'll make changes as the Scouts respond

412
00:25:48,000 --> 00:25:50,000
and they begin to get the idea,

413
00:25:50,000 --> 00:25:52,000
and you need to go at their speed.

414
00:25:52,000 --> 00:25:54,000
It'll be a little slower at times

415
00:25:54,000 --> 00:25:55,000
than you'd like to go

416
00:25:55,000 --> 00:25:57,000
and it will be a little faster

417
00:25:57,000 --> 00:25:59,000
if it should go at others

418
00:25:59,000 --> 00:26:01,000
And through this process.

419
00:26:01,000 --> 00:26:03,000
it's a good opportunity to test everything

420
00:26:03,000 --> 00:26:05,000
you think you know and understand about Scouting.

421
00:26:05,000 --> 00:26:07,000
Stay humble, keep studying.

422
00:26:07,000 --> 00:26:08,000
Like I said,

423
00:26:08,000 --> 00:26:10,000
sometimes it'll go a little faster

424
00:26:10,000 --> 00:26:11,000
than you thought it would,

425
00:26:11,000 --> 00:26:12,000
sometimes a little slower.

426
00:26:12,000 --> 00:26:14,000
Now, one important thing about this

427
00:26:14,000 --> 00:26:16,000
is that your older Scouts

428
00:26:16,000 --> 00:26:18,000
have been raised under very different

429
00:26:18,000 --> 00:26:19,000
adult leadership.

430
00:26:19,000 --> 00:26:21,000
You have to have some respect for this.

431
00:26:21,000 --> 00:26:23,000
Don't try to change them.

432
00:26:23,000 --> 00:26:25,000
You know what I mean.

433
00:26:25,000 --> 00:26:28,000
Don't impose a whole lot of changes on them.

434
00:26:28,000 --> 00:26:30,000
Don't tell them: well, you know

435
00:26:30,000 --> 00:26:31,000
everything you've done up till now.

436
00:26:31,000 --> 00:26:33,000
it was done the wrong way

437
00:26:33,000 --> 00:26:35,000
and we're going to start doing it the new way.

438
00:26:35,000 --> 00:26:37,000
You have to understand

439
00:26:37,000 --> 00:26:39,000
that our interest is always the individual Scout

440
00:26:39,000 --> 00:26:41,000
and it's not the fault of those older Scouts

441
00:26:41,000 --> 00:26:43,000
that the previous adults may have led them

442
00:26:43,000 --> 00:26:45,000
in less than an optimal direction.

443
00:26:45,000 --> 00:26:48,000
If you put yourself in their shoes,

444
00:26:48,000 --> 00:26:50,000
they're going to naturally be suspicious

445
00:26:50,000 --> 00:26:53,000
or even antagonistic towards a new Scoutmaster.

446
00:26:53,000 --> 00:26:55,000
It's pretty natural.

447
00:26:55,000 --> 00:26:57,000
So I'd make a point of sitting down with them

448
00:26:57,000 --> 00:26:59,000
individually and expressing your respect

449
00:26:59,000 --> 00:27:01,000
for who they are as Scouts

450
00:27:01,000 --> 00:27:03,000
and what they've accomplished.

451
00:27:03,000 --> 00:27:05,000
Ask them what they expect. to

452
00:27:05,000 --> 00:27:07,000
ask them what they expect will happen

453
00:27:07,000 --> 00:27:09,000
over the next few weeks and months

454
00:27:09,000 --> 00:27:11,000
as you take over a Scoutmaster

455
00:27:11,000 --> 00:27:12,000
and listen carefully,

456
00:27:12,000 --> 00:27:14,000
Ask what they'd like to take on

457
00:27:14,000 --> 00:27:16,000
about making changes

458
00:27:16,000 --> 00:27:17,000
and how you could help.

459
00:27:17,000 --> 00:27:18,000
What you'll find is

460
00:27:18,000 --> 00:27:20,000
is that you'll be working with a set of standards

461
00:27:20,000 --> 00:27:22,000
they learned from their previous Scouters

462
00:27:22,000 --> 00:27:24,000
and you don't want to monkey around with that too much

463
00:27:24,000 --> 00:27:26,000
or too quickly.

464
00:27:26,000 --> 00:27:28,000
As I said, this is about them

465
00:27:28,000 --> 00:27:30,000
and their individual interests.

466
00:27:30,000 --> 00:27:32,000
Now some of those older Scouts

467
00:27:32,000 --> 00:27:34,000
will surprise you with their energy

468
00:27:34,000 --> 00:27:35,000
in making changes.

469
00:27:35,000 --> 00:27:37,000
Some are going to hold back,

470
00:27:37,000 --> 00:27:39,000
Resist the temptation to offer judgments

471
00:27:39,000 --> 00:27:41,000
or in any way characterize

472
00:27:41,000 --> 00:27:43,000
what they are used to doing as bad,

473
00:27:43,000 --> 00:27:45,000
and that you have a better way.

474
00:27:45,000 --> 00:27:47,000
Talk to them and ask them questions.

475
00:27:47,000 --> 00:27:49,000
How about we try it this way?

476
00:27:49,000 --> 00:27:51,000
Does that sound good to you guys?

477
00:27:51,000 --> 00:27:53,000
I think maybe we could try it

478
00:27:53,000 --> 00:27:54,000
a different way.

479
00:27:54,000 --> 00:27:56,000
That's the spirit of the thing

480
00:27:56,000 --> 00:27:57,000
that I'm talking about,

481
00:27:57,000 --> 00:27:59,000
Because being a new Scoutmaster

482
00:27:59,000 --> 00:28:01,000
in an old troop is all about

483
00:28:01,000 --> 00:28:02,000
your perspective.

484
00:28:02,000 --> 00:28:04,000
You need to learn as much as you can

485
00:28:04,000 --> 00:28:06,000
about your Scouts before you start

486
00:28:06,000 --> 00:28:08,000
moving them in a new direction too quickly.

487
00:28:08,000 --> 00:28:10,000
Share your ambitions with them

488
00:28:10,000 --> 00:28:12,000
in a way where you're asking

489
00:28:12,000 --> 00:28:14,000
their permission to make changes

490
00:28:14,000 --> 00:28:16,000
rather than dictating

491
00:28:16,000 --> 00:28:17,000
those changes to them.

492
00:28:17,000 --> 00:28:19,000
Scouts will usually take

493
00:28:19,000 --> 00:28:21,000
to changes that you're going to make

494
00:28:21,000 --> 00:28:23,000
faster than the adults, and they'll surprise you

495
00:28:23,000 --> 00:28:25,000
with how well they embrace those changes,

496
00:28:25,000 --> 00:28:27,000
so long as they're respected

497
00:28:27,000 --> 00:28:29,000
and they have some sense that

498
00:28:29,000 --> 00:28:31,000
they're in control of the situation.

499
00:28:31,000 --> 00:28:33,000
Now I'm not giving you much in the way

500
00:28:33,000 --> 00:28:35,000
of specific program advice and direction,

501
00:28:35,000 --> 00:28:37,000
and that's actually because

502
00:28:37,000 --> 00:28:39,000
I would think that this is one

503
00:28:39,000 --> 00:28:41,000
of the least important aspects

504
00:28:41,000 --> 00:28:42,000
of the next few months.

505
00:28:42,000 --> 00:28:44,000
I know how large

506
00:28:44,000 --> 00:28:46,000
these program concerns loom

507
00:28:46,000 --> 00:28:47,000
right now.

508
00:28:47,000 --> 00:28:49,000
Where are we going to go camping?

509
00:28:49,000 --> 00:28:51,000
What are we going to do at meetings?

510
00:28:51,000 --> 00:28:53,000
How is this going to work? How is that going to work?

511
00:28:53,000 --> 00:28:55,000
But what you're doing for the next few months

512
00:28:55,000 --> 00:28:57,000
is observing and forming your perspective

513
00:28:57,000 --> 00:28:59,000
and leading from behind.

514
00:28:59,000 --> 00:29:01,000
I'll repeat again:

515
00:29:01,000 --> 00:29:03,000
You go at the Scouts pace.

516
00:29:03,000 --> 00:29:05,000
If all you end up doing is

517
00:29:05,000 --> 00:29:07,000
for a few meetings is play games,

518
00:29:07,000 --> 00:29:09,000
If all you do at a camping trip is set up,

519
00:29:09,000 --> 00:29:11,000
tense and cook, be happy with that

520
00:29:11,000 --> 00:29:13,000
and study your Scouts.

521
00:29:13,000 --> 00:29:15,000
Of course you're going to share some ideas

522
00:29:15,000 --> 00:29:17,000
and you're going to ask lots of questions

523
00:29:17,000 --> 00:29:19,000
that will help them figure out more things to do.

524
00:29:19,000 --> 00:29:21,000
So the program will

525
00:29:21,000 --> 00:29:23,000
reasonably take care of itself.

526
00:29:23,000 --> 00:29:25,000
You're going to have to work with them

527
00:29:25,000 --> 00:29:27,000
and make sure that the activities

528
00:29:27,000 --> 00:29:29,000
are planned and make sure that things are happening,

529
00:29:29,000 --> 00:29:31,000
But with the first few months,

530
00:29:31,000 --> 00:29:33,000
what you're really looking for is

531
00:29:33,000 --> 00:29:35,000
you're looking for understanding

532
00:29:35,000 --> 00:29:37,000
who they are, expressing

533
00:29:37,000 --> 00:29:39,000
some respect and asking their permission

534
00:29:39,000 --> 00:29:41,000
to make these changes.

535
00:29:41,000 --> 00:29:43,000
Now, when it comes to the adults that you're working with-

536
00:29:43,000 --> 00:29:45,000
you've heard me talk about this before-

537
00:29:45,000 --> 00:29:47,000
there's a difference between

538
00:29:47,000 --> 00:29:49,000
mentoring and guiding Scouts

539
00:29:49,000 --> 00:29:51,000
and leading adults. The adults in the troop

540
00:29:51,000 --> 00:29:53,000
picked you to be the Scoutmaster,

541
00:29:53,000 --> 00:29:55,000
so they picked you to lead them,

542
00:29:55,000 --> 00:29:57,000
So be very specific about what that means.

543
00:29:57,000 --> 00:29:59,000
Every new Scoutmaster

544
00:29:59,000 --> 00:30:01,000
is going to have some uncomfortable moments

545
00:30:01,000 --> 00:30:03,000
here and there with adults who were

546
00:30:03,000 --> 00:30:05,000
involved with the troop prior to them

547
00:30:05,000 --> 00:30:07,000
being the Scoutmaster,

548
00:30:07,000 --> 00:30:09,000
But usually a little good humor

549
00:30:09,000 --> 00:30:11,000
in a friendly manner, diffuses that type of situation,

550
00:30:11,000 --> 00:30:13,000
Especially if you're developing

551
00:30:13,000 --> 00:30:15,000
the attitude of service

552
00:30:15,000 --> 00:30:17,000
to the interest of all the Scouts

553
00:30:17,000 --> 00:30:19,000
in the troop together,

554
00:30:19,000 --> 00:30:21,000
all the Scouts in patrols together

555
00:30:21,000 --> 00:30:23,000
and each and every Scout individually.

556
00:30:23,000 --> 00:30:25,000
It's a lot of work

557
00:30:25,000 --> 00:30:27,000
to become a new Scoutmaster in an old troop

